            "type": "USINT"
          },
          {
            "name": "FREQUENCY",
            "type": "REAL"
          },
          {
            "name": "DUTY",
            "type": "REAL"
          },
          {
            "name": "ON_STOP",
            "type": "USINT"
          }
        ],
        "outputs": [
          {
            "name": "VALID",
            "type": "BOOL"
          },
          {
            "name": "ERROR",
            "type": "USINT"
          },
          {
            "name": "ERRORTEXT",
            "type": "STRING"
          }
        ],
        "inouts": [],
        "implementation": "cpp",
        "sourceFile": "PWM_OUT.cpp",
        "documentation": "Drives a hardware PWM channel of the Linux device (sysfs) at FREQUENCY (Hz) with DUTY (0 to 100 %). CHIP is the pwmchip number ('0'), its name ('pwmchip0') or its device name; CHANNEL counts from 0. The channel is set up outside the PLC scan and written only when a value changes; VALID is TRUE once it runs with the values asked for. When the block is no longer called (PLC stop, PLC error, new upload) the channel goes to ON_STOP: LIO_STOP_OFF (default, duty 0 and disabled) or LIO_STOP_HOLD. ENABLE FALSE turns the channel off, also one left running by LIO_STOP_HOLD before. A board may need its PWM driver switched on in its boot settings first.\nExample: fan(ENABLE := TRUE, CHIP := '0', CHANNEL := 0, FREQUENCY := 1000.0, DUTY := speed);"
      },
      {
        "name": "READ_VALUE",
        "inputs": [
